 (2026) (Count \n 2 \n1)\; reckless conduct with a dangerous weapon (Class 
 C)\, 17 -A M.R.S. §§ 211 \, \n1604(5)(A) (2026) (Count 2)\; possession o
 f a firearm by a prohibited person  \n(Class C)\, 15 M.R.S. §  393(1)(C)(
 1) (2026)  (Count 3) \; and criminal mischief \n(Class D)\, 17 -A M.R.S. 
 § 806 (1)(A) (2026) (Count 4).   The court entered a \njudgment of convic
 tion on all counts\, sentencing Ali to fifteen years ’ \nimprisonment wi
 th all but seven years suspended and four years of probation  \non Count 1
  and concurrent sentences on the remaining counts. \n [¶3]  Ali appealed 
 \, arguing that the court’s failure to merge the \nconvictions for eleva
 ted aggravated assault  (Count 1) and reckless conduct \nwith a dangerous 
 weapon (Count 2) violated the double jeopardy clause of the \nU.S. Constit
 ution.  Ali\, 2025 ME 30\, ¶¶ 10\, 16\, 334 A.3d 657.  Upon concluding \
 nthat the court should have merged the two convictions because “the jury
  could \nhave based its verdict for bot\nhttps://www.courts.maine.gov/cour
 ts/sjc/lawcourt/2026/26me078.pdf
